-- Q3714: Privacy Requirements and Data Protection [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Notify the supervisory authority within 72 hours if the breach poses risk","No notification is required because no patient data was actually exfiltrated","Notify only the affected patients directly within a 24-hour window","Wait until the full investigation concludes before making any notifications"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GDPR Article 33, the hospital must notify the supervisory authority within 72 hours if the breach is likely to result in risk to individuals'' rights. Article 4(12) defines breach to include unavailability, so the 36-hour ransomware lockout qualifies as reportable.
Wrong (B): GDPR Article 4(12) explicitly includes unavailability of personal data as a breach type, so the absence of exfiltration does not eliminate the notification obligation.
Wrong (C): Patient notification under Article 34 applies only when there is high risk, and the 72-hour supervisory authority notification comes first.
Wrong (D): GDPR imposes a 72-hour deadline specifically to prevent organizations from delaying notification until investigations conclude.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3714;

-- Q3738: Privacy Requirements and Data Protection [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The accuracy principle requiring data correctness","The storage limitation principle restricting retention periods","The data protection by default principle for privacy settings","The accountability principle requiring documented compliance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): GDPR Article 25 requires that default settings maximize privacy, meaning only data necessary for each specific purpose is processed. Users should not need to take affirmative action to protect their data, so auto-enrolling users into location sharing with advertisers violates this principle.
